            },
            {
                "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                "word": "roper",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "e6541da3-aba9-4acf-9f5e-97406a890025",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "ropers"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7f08f0f6-54bf-3cb9-8c52-5968b2e26d6a",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Agent noun of rope; one who uses a rope, especially one who throws a lariat or lasso."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "agent",
                            "form-of"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fbff6adf-8752-4a2d-91a8-910319609306",
                                "sense_id": "7f08f0f6-54bf-3cb9-8c52-5968b2e26d6a",
                                "text": "I'm a roper — mostly tie-down, but I do some team, too."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b88a3de-078b-42f4-8d7d-00ec76b7f96b",
                                "sense_id": "7f08f0f6-54bf-3cb9-8c52-5968b2e26d6a",
                                "text": "Instead of taking to the open and falling a prey to a roper, the calf lunged sideways and went under the horse-pasture fence."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b850ac1c-ebaa-4ea4-b149-8f1c64100a53",
                                "sense_id": "7f08f0f6-54bf-3cb9-8c52-5968b2e26d6a",
                                "text": "After a roper flanks a calf (picks it up and lays it down on the ground oh so very gently), he/she decides to wrap one time or two times prior to the Hooey (the Hooey is the half hitch that locks your tie together)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2c9d289b-9b67-3931-abb9-64dbbe6a79fa",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A ropemaker (a maker of ropes)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dated"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e23627c-27bd-4d98-af11-d632efa99f1d",
                                "sense_id": "2c9d289b-9b67-3931-abb9-64dbbe6a79fa",
                                "text": "But Gideon Giles was no common man, although a roper."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c6744566-f5d9-4428-ba2b-2f5271a9723f",
                                "sense_id": "2c9d289b-9b67-3931-abb9-64dbbe6a79fa",
                                "text": "A roper's wife, for instance, was able to fool her incredibly gullible husband while having an affair with a prior literally right under the roper's nose, the guilty pair having sex while they lay together in the same bed as the sleeping roper."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "de9514e2-abd4-3476-8e47-fe8290f9f6d4",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "One who ropes goods; a packer."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "daf2ae68-66dc-4d54-907d-d8ccc6858c54",
                                "sense_id": "de9514e2-abd4-3476-8e47-fe8290f9f6d4",
                                "text": "I have seen 50 to 60 men doing this work, and the men vied with each other to see which could cap or rope the best; and if a bale was turned off from the capper that did not look well, some of the others would criticise it, and the same with ropers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4c5f1241-8bda-4c97-88fc-d56344192998",
                                "sense_id": "de9514e2-abd4-3476-8e47-fe8290f9f6d4",
                                "text": "About thirty per cent of all the women employed in the packing plant are in the sausage department. They work as linkers, tie-ers, ropers and hangers; help in the preparation of the raw materials, mix spices, and wash pans."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "28849fd0-f7bb-448d-a2d3-0cdc4e0fab5c",
                                "sense_id": "de9514e2-abd4-3476-8e47-fe8290f9f6d4",
                                "text": "The diggers pay their \"ropers\" $10/bu for \"littlenecks\" and $1/bu for \"cherrystones.\" On good days, each digger may gross almost $600. After paying the roper, this leaves him with about $500 a day before other expenses are taken out."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cdb2735c-9b1a-32ae-8a68-183390572a3e",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of outside man (“accomplice who locates a mark to be swindled by a confidence trickster”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d256bb2-3872-46db-90db-c08ba42050b6",
                                "sense_id": "cdb2735c-9b1a-32ae-8a68-183390572a3e",
                                "text": "The \"roper\" will inform the mark that such horses can't be picked out of the Form; what one needs is inside information."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "51cdd3f1-e238-4175-bd4f-544de894807a",
                                "sense_id": "cdb2735c-9b1a-32ae-8a68-183390572a3e",
                                "text": "Here's how it usually goes: You're forced to rely on the only tow truck driver in the entire county, who turns out to be a roper for the local inbred family of serial killers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c9511cc8-028d-44ef-834a-954e8e210423",
                                "sense_id": "cdb2735c-9b1a-32ae-8a68-183390572a3e",
                                "text": "For example, one person may have a specialty in cooling off the mark, while another is able to lure in the mark with ease; these people may be referred to as the “roper” or the “outside man”."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ae3fcb75-44fc-4de9-bbe4-a68cb8c093df",
                                "sense_id": "cdb2735c-9b1a-32ae-8a68-183390572a3e",
                                "text": "What have you got—a store con? Shall you be needing a roper?"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "defee0c4-2972-319f-9739-99c63175ca98",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A person hired by a gambling establishment to locate potential customers and bring them in."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5305bb23-0057-4b1a-be2a-fdc2994336e3",
                                "sense_id": "defee0c4-2972-319f-9739-99c63175ca98",
                                "text": "Any person who, in this city, lives idly and is a gambler, or roper, steerer or capper for any gambling house or room, or any gambling game, or who lives idly and has the reputation of being a gambler, or roper, steerer or capper for any gambling house or room, or any gambling game, shall be considered and treated as a vagrant."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "499b1ded-9811-4721-8177-0add94c4ae6a",
                                "sense_id": "defee0c4-2972-319f-9739-99c63175ca98",
                                "text": "Ropers like the one he'd sent packing would never steer him to such a highclass joint. So he kept giving the ropers the shove while he watched to see arriving customers point the way."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "65024b33-7d73-46cc-b554-88c2235d32e3",
                                "sense_id": "defee0c4-2972-319f-9739-99c63175ca98",
                                "text": "His old partner Allen Jones hired him as a roper for his gambling operation located across the street from the St. Charles Hotel."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "188edba1-6ecc-460a-bbe2-41151ed8f803",
                                "sense_id": "defee0c4-2972-319f-9739-99c63175ca98",
                                "text": "He first appeared among St. Louis sporting men as a “roper” and venturesome player against the bank."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "13026978-0c03-36de-9dd5-485d56ac4237",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An undercover informer."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8bce9644-2189-4126-b3c2-94f42dd98a6d",
                                "sense_id": "13026978-0c03-36de-9dd5-485d56ac4237",
                                "text": "Supposing in a plant on a job a roper roped a man, who was, let us say, employed by the company, and maybe a member of the union, how much would he get after he was roped?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6db12c7a-e948-4837-8b05-a565bfbc9b2f",
                                "sense_id": "13026978-0c03-36de-9dd5-485d56ac4237",
                                "text": "Such an operative might also be the contact for one or more \"missionaries\" or labour spies. The roper who was sent for was Louis M. Wendell."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "07e01ad5-5b4f-438f-8d3f-0d47adf0fd00",
                                "sense_id": "13026978-0c03-36de-9dd5-485d56ac4237",
                                "text": "By 1876, however, he was well launched on a career as a professional informer, or “roper,” for the Secret Service."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a1498ec3-cdbf-33c2-b6be-2a6b83b20e01",
                        "entry_id": "f7b83ba0-6908-32b8-9760-d4c9a1df6948",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of a variety of monsters with tentacles that they use to capture victims."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ffb14c29-b1fb-4ca4-96b4-ecbd201d873d",
                                "sense_id": "a1498ec3-cdbf-33c2-b6be-2a6b83b20e01",
                                "text": "The party ran into a statue of a roper, which somehow attacked them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fa0073ab-113b-43c2-9cce-e453087ae875",
                                "sense_id": "a1498ec3-cdbf-33c2-b6be-2a6b83b20e01",
                                "text": "Stone ropers are distant relatives of the common roper though the two races are do not (as far as sages know) associate with one another."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1bebe0ae-9f6e-40f3-a26d-5e75c843c648",
                                "sense_id": "a1498ec3-cdbf-33c2-b6be-2a6b83b20e01",
                                "text": "I understand this to mean that passive Wisdom (Perception)—and even Searching—will never reveal a roper for what it is as long as it holds still. Its Stealth skill comes into play only if it's moving. Thus, a stationary roper takes its opponents by surprise, as long as its eye is closed and its tendrils retracted until it strikes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "902ed3e1-288e-4c5b-b9a3-771ea5be7c2c",
                                "sense_id": "a1498ec3-cdbf-33c2-b6be-2a6b83b20e01",
                                "text": "Moments ago, vines had shot from the ground to lash at us; I'd thought at first that they were the tentacles of a worm or roper monster, but the leaves revealed that we were dealing·with some kind of plant creature."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
